  "notes": "Organized Noize's board: a played southern-soul band that a rap record sits on, and the one thing in the whole Southern club-rap container that has real harmony. Four parameters carry it and none of them are crunk's. (1) The harmony moves — ii–v–i, minor 7/9 stacks, a plagal IV–I tag and the documented I–IV–V–vi with the deceptive cadence — over 4-bar loops, so `extensions.triad` is 0.22 where crunk is 0.92 and `progressionFamilies` is eight families deep where crunk is a one-chord drone. (2) The bass is a player, not a sub: `rhythm` is `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.5, over a 24-semitone register with M2/M6/m2 passing tones and walking `cellShapes` — the chromatic approach the research names. (3) The kit is live under programmed snares: ghosts on almost every e and a, `offGridMs` behind the grid, tambourine and congas as standing layers, and `lockTo808` down at 0.3 because the kick and the 808 are two instruments here rather than trap's one. (4) The sung hook is a real second voice, so `countermelody.fillGapsOnly` is false — it sings through the lead rather than around it — at `densityRatio` 0.62. ⚠ **Confidence is medium and the inferred parameters are the tempo band and the swing.** The archetype exists as a *recommendation* in the research's crunk Assignment notes; every tempo it rests on except the 159 BPM 2003 anchor is marked [LOW-CONF] (Big Boi, Goodie Mob, CeeLo, Organized Noize's own 70–92), so 68–100/mode 84 is a lane reading. `swing.amount` 0.57 is INFERRED outright — the research says \"8ths/16ths with swing\" and gives no number; it sits between funk's 0.56 and neo-soul's 0.63 because this is a played band and not a Dilla pocket. ⛔ **The research's metric irregularity is only half-expressible.** It documents a meter change on the fourth measure of each six-measure phrase, 22 quarter-note beats per phrase, and asks the generator for mid-phrase meter changes. The schema has no time signature and no per-phrase meter, so what is authored is the reachable half: `melody.phraseBars` carries 6 alongside 4, 8 and 2 so phrases come in odd lengths, and `arrangement.sectionBars` gives ranges rather than fixed 16s. Every bar is still 4/4 — the signature figure itself is not in this file. ⚠ **The 808 is `counter_riff`, and that is an engine limit read honestly.** The research is explicit that the booty-bass 808 is added *beneath* the played bass rather than instead of it, but the engine's low end is one part or the other: `role: \"bassline\"` would silence the played bass, which is the part this genre is about. `counter_riff` is the only role that lets both sound, and it is given a low slide probability and no distortion so it reads as reinforcement rather than as a trap 808. ⚠ **Subtraction is authored as an absent fill and a changing part list.** The trio's stated method was to \"go in and take some stuff out\", so `drums.fills.fillBeforeSection` is false — a section is not announced by a turn-around — and the `arrangement.sectionRules` change the part list section by section, so a section arrives by an instrument entering and leaves by one dropping. (`arrangement.transitions.fillBeforeSection` is deliberately *not* authored here: `arrange.rs` never reads it, so setting it would have looked like the claim without being it.) The 2-bar/4-bar `drums.fills` cycle is this batch's fingerprint and is busier than these records turn a bar over; it is the first number to lower if the model ever sounds like it is announcing itself. ⚠ `borrowed.V` at 0.26 is a reading of \"secondary dominants\" in the CeeLo entry rather than a counted figure. **Modes:** the four wings the research names — `soul band` is the 1993–98 played Organized Noize era, `dark church` is Goodie Mob's political wing counted half-time with the backbeat on 3 (the second of the two placements the research asks for), `sung hook` is the CeeLo/Sleepy Brown gospel-soul harmony described as the most functional in the section, and `rave` is the documented post-2000 jump to \"faster, more chaotic tempos to reflect rave culture\" that the 159 BPM single anchors. ⚠ **`chords.voicing.register` floor raised from 45 to 52**, which is where every played-band lane in the roster puts it: the chord floor sits ON the bass top, not under it — `funk` [28,52]/[52,79], `g-funk` [28,48]/[48,76], `neo-soul` [30,43]/[43,77], `rnb-2000s` [31,46]/[46,74]. At 45 against a bass reaching 52 an **open** voicing spread upward from A2 straight through the register the played bass works in, so the comp could sound *below* the bass line — which is what `no_model_puts_its_bass_where_its_chords_live` caught. ⛔ The bass register is the one that is load-bearing here and it is unchanged: 28–52 is the 24 semitones this file’s own note names, and the walking `cellShapes` and chromatic approach tones need every one of them. `funk` is the nearest lane — the same live southern band, the same bass register — so its floor is the one taken.",
